{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Moses answered and said, But behold, they will not believe me, nor hearken to my voice.</b> At that moment Moses spoke inappropriately — HaShem had said to him, 'And they shall hearken to your voice' (Exodus 3:18), yet he said, <b>But behold, they will not believe me</b>. Immediately the Holy One, blessed be He, answered him according to his own line of reasoning and gave him signs corresponding to his words — this is the language of 'And these are the names Rabbah' (3:12). And R. Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Exodus 4:1) said, according to the plain sense, that HaShem said the elders would believe in him, but He did not mention that the people would believe; or that they would hearken to his voice but perhaps would not believe with a whole heart. But this is not correct. Rather, it is possible to say that 'And they shall hearken to your voice' is not a promise but a command — 'they shall hearken to your voice,' for it is fitting that they should hearken, 'and you shall come, you and the elders of Israel, to the king of Egypt.' And likewise, 'And the Egyptians shall hear that you have lifted up your power, and they shall say to the inhabitants of the land' (Numbers 14:13–14) — it is fitting that it should be so. And likewise, 'By this you shall know that I am YHWH' (Exodus 7:17) — fitting, not a guarantee of fulfillment. And likewise, 'And they shall believe the voice of the latter sign' later in this very passage (Exodus 4:8), and many similar cases. But the correct interpretation in my eyes is that 'And they shall hearken to your voice' means to come with you to the king and say to him, 'The Elohim of the Hebrews has called upon us' — for what would they lose? Now, HaShem had informed him that the king of Egypt would not let them go. Therefore Moses said, <b>But behold, they will not believe me</b> — after they see that Pharaoh does not let them go — <b>nor will they hearken</b> to my voice at all, <b>for they will say, YHWH has not appeared to you</b>: for if you were the messenger of HaShem, Pharaoh would not defy His word. Or they will say: The great HaShem, in the attribute of mercy, has not appeared to you to perform signs and wonders for us as you have said, for you are not greater than the Patriarchs; and therefore Pharaoh did not hearken — for if Pharaoh had believed your words, we would depart in any case, and our sins do not separate us from [His] mercy."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {0 "The last phrase 'our sins do not separate us from [His] mercy' reads וְאֵין עֲוֹנֹתֵינוּ מַבְדִילִין בֵּינֵינוּ וּבֵין הָרַחֲמִים; the bracketed 'His' is supplied to render the construct הרחמים, which could also mean 'the mercies' or 'the attribute of mercy' — left ambiguous as in the original."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:02:11.044737314Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}